WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — agency (180s) Narrator Joy

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.142
First, an agent must aim at a goal,

2
00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:07.197
act on the world, and stay within an energy budget.

3
00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:10.907
Second, physics prices every decision,

4
00:00:10.907 --> 00:00:17.373
creating one optimal pace between slow idle drain and frantic switching waste.

5
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:25.266
Third, among systems that finish a task correctly, the smarter one is whichever spent less energy.

6
00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:33.098
Today's AI scoreboards celebrate whichever system gets the highest raw score, ignoring whether one answer

7
00:00:33.098 --> 00:00:39.331
burned a thousand watts or a thousandth of a watt. Pouring more electricity into a machine

8
00:00:39.331 --> 00:00:47.226
can buy capability, yet true intelligence means solving the exact same problem parsimoniously with minimal energy.

9
00:00:47.786 --> 00:00:54.497
Imagine balancing an upright broomstick on your palm while walking on a strict calorie budget. Wait

10
00:00:54.497 --> 00:01:01.749
too long between corrections and the stick tips away while baseline metabolism drains your reserves; twitch

11
00:01:01.749 --> 00:01:07.850
too frantically and every rapid muscle reset dumps extra heat into the air for tiny gains.

12
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:17.005
The framework stacks three layers in a fixed order. First comes eligibility: fix a discriminating prediction beforehand,

13
00:01:17.005 --> 00:01:25.171
physically push on the outside world rather than editing your own sensors, update internal memory from the result,

14
00:01:25.171 --> 00:01:33.122
and stay inside your joule grant or log a refusal receipt. Next come speed limits, then hindsight intelligence.

15
00:01:33.682 --> 00:01:41.402
Every time a machine compares an outcome and resets memory over a closed cycle, physics charges a minimum

16
00:01:41.402 --> 00:01:49.562
heat floor per erased bit while idle circuits leak steady baseline watts. Dividing useful task velocity by both

17
00:01:49.562 --> 00:01:57.869
costs combined traces a smooth arch peaking at one optimal action pace where marginal drag equals baseline drain.

18
00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:06.275
In this three-dimensional landscape, the flat lower plane marks nature's minimum heat price for updating a single

19
00:02:06.275 --> 00:02:12.801
bit of memory at room temperature. Steep walls on either side show slow idle battery drain and

20
00:02:12.801 --> 00:02:21.063
frantic high-speed heat waste, framing one golden ridge where verified progress per joule reaches its physical maximum.

21
00:02:21.623 --> 00:02:30.728
Across pre-registered forty-seed puzzle benchmarks on two computers, moving receipt writing inside the budget guard reduced overspends

22
00:02:30.728 --> 00:02:39.154
to zero across ten thousand eight hundred runs. Requiring task completion first stopped a cheap-failing random baseline from

23
00:02:39.154 --> 00:02:46.425
gaming raw bit rates, while our threshold selector closed more tasks than a fixed cascade at loose budgets.

24
00:02:46.985 --> 00:02:56.028
Scientific honesty requires reporting every failed check alongside every success. Tight-budget selector gains from our first

25
00:02:56.028 --> 00:03:04.925
exploratory run did not replicate, shared tasks used equal energy, and built-in Linux chip counters missed sub-millisecond

26
00:03:04.925 --> 00:03:13.385
steps completely, proving why dedicated quiet hosts and external wall or circuit-board power sensors must come next.
